WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.095
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book one.

2
00:00:05.395 --> 00:00:08.874
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.174 --> 00:00:15.518
Unit eighteen: Academic Conferences. Today you will structure accounts with sequencing language.

4
00:00:15.818 --> 00:00:18.517
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.467 --> 00:00:22.746
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.583 --> 00:00:24.608
Conference.

7
00:00:25.617 --> 00:00:29.799
Conference means a formal gathering where researchers present and discuss work.

8
00:00:30.383 --> 00:00:33.416
The conference attracted delegates from forty countries.

9
00:00:34.017 --> 00:00:36.588
Your turn. Say: Conference.

10
00:00:39.433 --> 00:00:40.516
Conference.

11
00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:42.147
Keynote.

12
00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:46.461
Keynote means the principal address that sets the theme of an event.

13
00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:49.874
The keynote challenged conventional thinking on AI.

14
00:00:50.483 --> 00:00:52.915
Your turn. Say: Keynote.

15
00:00:55.767 --> 00:00:56.675
Keynote.

16
00:00:57.417 --> 00:00:58.116
Panel.

17
00:00:59.117 --> 00:01:02.927
Panel means a group of experts discussing a topic before an audience.

18
00:01:03.517 --> 00:01:06.108
The panel debated the ethics of data collection.

19
00:01:06.717 --> 00:01:09.102
Your turn. Say: Panel.

20
00:01:11.967 --> 00:01:12.631
Panel.

21
00:01:13.383 --> 00:01:14.593
Poster session.

22
00:01:15.583 --> 00:01:20.532
Poster session means an event where research is displayed visually and discussed informally.

23
00:01:21.117 --> 00:01:24.637
Early-career researchers often begin at poster sessions.

24
00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:28.030
Your turn. Say: Poster session.

25
00:01:30.867 --> 00:01:32.146
Poster session.

26
00:01:32.883 --> 00:01:33.919
Networking.

27
00:01:34.917 --> 00:01:38.925
Networking means building professional relationships for mutual benefit.

28
00:01:39.517 --> 00:01:42.294
Networking during breaks led to a joint grant.

29
00:01:42.883 --> 00:01:45.629
Your turn. Say: Networking.

30
00:01:48.467 --> 00:01:49.537
Networking.

31
00:01:50.283 --> 00:01:51.401
Proceedings.

32
00:01:52.417 --> 00:01:56.460
Proceedings means the published collection of papers presented at a conference.

33
00:01:57.050 --> 00:01:59.363
Selected papers appear in the proceedings.

34
00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:02.997
Your turn. Say: Proceedings.

35
00:02:05.833 --> 00:02:06.892
Proceedings.

36
00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:08.674
Abstract.

37
00:02:09.683 --> 00:02:14.109
Abstract means a concise summary of a paper, submitted for selection.

38
00:02:14.717 --> 00:02:17.599
Only thirty percent of abstracts were accepted.

39
00:02:18.183 --> 00:02:20.766
Your turn. Say: Abstract.

40
00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:24.717
Abstract.

41
00:02:25.483 --> 00:02:26.601
Discussant.

42
00:02:27.617 --> 00:02:31.602
Discussant means a scholar assigned to comment critically on presented papers.

43
00:02:32.217 --> 00:02:34.924
The discussant identified a flaw in the sampling.

44
00:02:35.517 --> 00:02:38.239
Your turn. Say: Discussant.

45
00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:42.240
Discussant.

46
00:02:43.033 --> 00:02:46.231
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:47.083 --> 00:02:52.104
Visal, you presented at the regional youth research conference. Talk me through the day.

48
00:02:53.217 --> 00:03:00.850
First the keynote, then panels, finally a poster session. The keynote on climate migration set the tone brilliantly.

49
00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:04.008
How did your own presentation go?

50
00:03:05.117 --> 00:03:15.246
Initially I was nervous. Then the discussant asked a challenging question about my sample, which actually improved the discussion.

51
00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:18.233
What was the most valuable part?

52
00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:25.718
Ultimately, the networking. I met two students who want to replicate my study in their provinces.

53
00:03:26.867 --> 00:03:28.425
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:29.283 --> 00:03:32.990
Grammar focus: Sequencing language (first, then, finally).

55
00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:34.546
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:38.712
First the keynote, then panels, finally a poster session.

57
00:03:39.617 --> 00:03:44.034
Initially I was nervous. Subsequently, the discussion improved.

58
00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:50.514
First, abstracts are submitted; then they are reviewed; finally, speakers are selected.

59
00:03:51.467 --> 00:04:02.167
'First, then, finally' order steps. More formal alternatives are 'initially, subsequently, ultimately'. Note: 'finally' marks the last item, while 'at last' expresses relief after waiting.

60
00:04:02.917 --> 00:04:06.962
Be careful! Don't say: Firstly the keynote, after the panels.

61
00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:11.271
Say: First the keynote, then the panels.

62
00:04:11.571 --> 00:04:13.443
Use 'then' to show the next step.

63
00:04:14.233 --> 00:04:16.828
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:17.633 --> 00:04:20.004
Inside an Academic Conference.

65
00:04:20.304 --> 00:04:26.066
Academic conferences remain central to scholarly life, despite the growth of online communication.

66
00:04:27.067 --> 00:04:34.163
A typical programme follows a familiar rhythm: first the keynote, then panels, finally a poster session.

67
00:04:35.167 --> 00:04:41.218
Initially, presenters summarise their work; subsequently, a discussant offers critical commentary.

68
00:04:42.233 --> 00:04:46.775
Arguably, the most productive exchanges occur informally, during networking breaks.

69
00:04:47.767 --> 00:04:52.587
Ultimately, many conference papers are revised and published in the proceedings.

70
00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:58.456
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:59.317 --> 00:05:02.953
First the keynote, blank panels, finally a poster session.

72
00:05:03.253 --> 00:05:05.885
A, than. B, then. C, after.

73
00:05:09.433 --> 00:05:11.096
The answer is B: then.

74
00:05:12.250 --> 00:05:14.586
Which set is the most formal sequence?

75
00:05:14.886 --> 00:05:21.570
A, first, next, last. B, initially, subsequently, ultimately. C, at first, after, at last.

76
00:05:25.133 --> 00:05:28.793
The answer is B: initially, subsequently, ultimately.

77
00:05:29.950 --> 00:05:33.610
The scholar who comments critically on presented papers is the blank.

78
00:05:33.910 --> 00:05:37.586
A, keynote. B, discussant. C, delegate.

79
00:05:41.133 --> 00:05:43.562
The answer is B: discussant.

80
00:05:44.717 --> 00:05:49.250
'We waited three hours; blank the speaker arrived.' (expressing relief)

81
00:05:49.550 --> 00:05:53.389
A, at last. B, finally. C, ultimately.

82
00:05:56.933 --> 00:05:58.991
The answer is A: at last.

83
00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:04.321
According to the reading, where do the most productive exchanges often occur?

84
00:06:04.621 --> 00:06:09.632
A, during keynotes. B, during networking breaks. C, in the proceedings.

85
00:06:13.167 --> 00:06:16.188
The answer is B: during networking breaks.

86
00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:20.842
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:21.683 --> 00:06:25.726
What do you think is the value of attending an academic conference in person?

88
00:06:42.517 --> 00:06:43.820
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:44.120 --> 00:07:20.196
In my view, attending in person offers something online events cannot replicate. First, you hear the latest research before it is published. Then, you can ask questions and receive immediate feedback on your own work. Finally, and perhaps most importantly, informal conversations often lead to future collaborations. Admittedly, travel is expensive and not environmentally friendly, so hybrid events have real advantages. Nevertheless, the personal connections made in person are hard to replace.

90
00:07:20.496 --> 00:07:22.310
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:23.217 --> 00:07:28.226
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and sequencing language (first, then, finally).

92
00:07:28.526 --> 00:07:32.130
Today's value is community. Help the people in your town.

93
00:07:32.430 --> 00:07:35.071
See you in Unit nineteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:35.371 --> 00:07:37.965
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

